the fourth clarification; research R-56). Made on feat/004-intake against main a964494; apply after P-7
(docs/merge-requests/2026-10-27-feat-004-intake.md). git apply skips this header.

It adds two files:
- tools/gates/outline_check.py (mode 100755), standard library only, which the Author runs directly from main's
  checkout, with the working folder inside the photos folder; its shebang and re-exec guard give it PY_HOST's flags.
  Two forms: --from-svg <trace>.svg --sign ... (writes <sign>.outline.json from each <path>'s d and the root's
  viewBox, says how many other elements it left behind and how many were images, refuses a path under a transform,
  a document type declaration, and an output name with a folder), and <sign>.outline.json (checks a file). Rules O1
  to O10 as §10 gives them, paths 1 to 512 strings and at most 262,144 characters. Output: `<rule> <file name>:
  <reason>` lines, then `outline-check: PASS: <file name>, <n> paths, sha256 <64 hex>` or `outline-check: <n>
  findings`, never a value or a path. Exit 0 PASS, 1 findings, 2 a file or the SVG it can't read.
- tools/gates/test/test_outline_check.py: §10's cases, every file built at run time.

Regenerated after T041's review (part 2, I-4 and nits 1 to 3): the conversion copies only what the Author sees drawn
in the tracing tool. It refuses, exit 2, nothing written, the report naming counts alone: a path under defs,
clipPath, mask, symbol, pattern, marker, metadata, switch, foreignObject, or a foreign element; a hidden element
(display, visibility, or opacity), or what sits under one; a use or a nested svg; a style with a transform; a style
element that can hide, clip, or bring in what it draws; a clip or a mask. It reads a regular file only (never a link,
a FIFO, or a device) of at most MAX_SVG_BYTES, 16 MiB, in UTF-8 with no byte-order mark, no other declared encoding,
no document type declaration or processing instruction, and no element nested deeper than 256; it walks the tree
without recursion. A write that fails exits 2, naming no path, and leaves no temporary file.

Again after the re-review (part 2): a style's transform is the transform property, read from the parsed
declarations, or `transform:` in a style element, so Inkscape's text-transform:none (Object to Path, Stroke to Path)
converts; a path its paint leaves undrawn (no fill: none, transparent, or fill-opacity 0; and no stroke: none,
absent, transparent, of opacity 0, or of width 0; fill and stroke inherited) is refused, as is a style element with
fill none; and the size cap's refusal says to delete the embedded photo before saving.

